{"instruction": "write a swift function to sort an array", "output": "Here's a Swift function to sort an array:\n\n```swift\nfunc sortArray<T: Comparable>(_ array: [T]) -> [T] {\n    return array.sorted()\n}\n\n// Usage examples\nlet numbers = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9]\nlet sorted = sortArray(numbers)\nprint(sorted)  // [1, 1, 3, 4, 5, 9]\n\n// Sort in place\nvar mutableArray = numbers\nmutableArray.sort()\n\n// Custom sorting\nlet descending = numbers.sorted { $0 > $1 }\n```\n\nKey points:\n• sorted() creates new array\n• sort() modifies in place\n• Generic function works with any Comparable type"}

{"instruction": "create a python function to reverse a string", "output": "Here's a Python function to reverse a string:\n\n```python\ndef reverse_string(s: str) -> str:\n    return s[::-1]\n\n# Alternative methods\ndef reverse_manual(s: str) -> str:\n    return ''.join(reversed(s))\n\ndef reverse_loop(s: str) -> str:\n    result = ''\n    for char in s:\n        result = char + result\n    return result\n\n# Usage\ntext = \"Hello, World!\"\nprint(reverse_string(text))  # !dlroW ,olleH\n```\n\nThe [::-1] slice is the most Pythonic way."}

{"instruction": "write javascript async function to fetch data", "output": "Here's a JavaScript async function to fetch data:\n\n```javascript\nasync function fetchData(url) {\n    try {\n        const response = await fetch(url);\n        \n        if (!response.ok) {\n            throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);\n        }\n        \n        const data = await response.json();\n        return data;\n    } catch (error) {\n        console.error('Fetch error:', error);\n        throw error;\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage\nfetchData('https://api.example.com/data')\n    .then(data => console.log(data))\n    .catch(err => console.error(err));\n\n// Or with async/await\nasync function main() {\n    const data = await fetchData('https://api.example.com/data');\n    console.log(data);\n}\n```"}

# EXPLANATION EXAMPLES
{"instruction": "explain async await in swift", "output": "**Async/Await in Swift**\n\nAsync/await is Swift's modern concurrency feature that makes asynchronous code look synchronous.\n\n**Key Concepts:**\n• `async` - Marks a function as asynchronous\n• `await` - Suspends execution until async operation completes\n• Non-blocking - Other code can run while waiting\n\n**Example:**\n```swift\n// Async function\nfunc fetchUser() async throws -> User {\n    let url = URL(string: \"https://api.example.com/user\")!\n    let (data, _) = try await URLSession.shared.data(from: url)\n    return try JSONDecoder().decode(User.self, from: data)\n}\n\n// Calling async function\nTask {\n    do {\n        let user = try await fetchUser()\n        print(\"User: \\(user.name)\")\n    } catch {\n        print(\"Error: \\(error)\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n✓ Cleaner than callbacks\n✓ Better error handling\n✓ Structured concurrency\n✓ Easier to read and maintain"}

{"instruction": "what is a closure", "output": "**Closures Explained**\n\nA closure is a self-contained block of functionality that can be passed around and used in your code.\n\n**Key Features:**\n• Capture values from surrounding context\n• First-class citizens (can be stored, passed, returned)\n• Often used for callbacks and functional programming\n\n**Swift Example:**\n```swift\n// Full syntax\nlet greeting = { (name: String) -> String in\n    return \"Hello, \\(name)!\"\n}\n\n// Shortened\nlet short = { name in \"Hello, \\(name)!\" }\nlet shorter = { \"Hello, \\($0)!\" }\n\n// Capturing values\nfunc makeCounter() -> () -> Int {\n    var count = 0\n    return {\n        count += 1\n        return count\n    }\n}\n\nlet counter = makeCounter()\nprint(counter())  // 1\nprint(counter())  // 2\n```\n\n**Common Uses:**\n• map, filter, reduce\n• Completion handlers\n• Animation blocks\n• Button actions"}

{"instruction": "explain optionals in swift", "output": "**Swift Optionals**\n\nOptionals represent a value that might be absent (nil).\n\n**Declaration:**\n```swift\nvar name: String? = \"Alice\"  // Optional String\nname = nil  // OK\n\nvar age: Int? = 25\n```\n\n**Unwrapping:**\n```swift\n// 1. Optional binding (safe)\nif let name = name {\n    print(\"Hello, \\(name)\")\n}\n\n// 2. Guard (early exit)\nguard let name = name else {\n    return\n}\n\n// 3. Nil coalescing\nlet displayName = name ?? \"Guest\"\n\n// 4. Optional chaining\nlet count = name?.count\n\n// 5. Force unwrap (dangerous!)\nlet forced = name!  // Crashes if nil\n```\n\n**Best Practices:**\n✓ Use optional binding or guard\n✓ Avoid force unwrapping\n✓ Use ?? for defaults\n✓ Use optional chaining for nested access"}

# DEBUGGING EXAMPLES
{"instruction": "help debug memory leak swift", "output": "**Debugging Memory Leaks in Swift**\n\n**Common Causes:**\n\n1. **Retain Cycles**\n```swift\n// BAD - Creates cycle\nclass MyClass {\n    var closure: (() -> Void)?\n    func setup() {\n        closure = {\n            self.doSomething()  // Strong reference\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD - Break cycle\nclass MyClass {\n    var closure: (() -> Void)?\n    func setup() {\n        closure = { [weak self] in\n            self?.doSomething()\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n2. **Delegate Patterns**\n```swift\n// Always use weak for delegates\nprotocol MyDelegate: AnyObject {\n    func didUpdate()\n}\n\nclass MyClass {\n    weak var delegate: MyDelegate?  // Important!\n}\n```\n\n**Detection:**\n• Use Instruments (Leaks tool)\n• Check allocation patterns\n• Review closure captures\n• Verify delegate relationships\n\n**Rules:**\n✓ Use [weak self] in closures\n✓ weak delegates\n✓ unowned when guaranteed to exist"}

{"instruction": "fix undefined variable error", "output": "**Fixing Undefined Variable Errors**\n\n**Common Causes:**\n\n1. **Variable not declared**\n```swift\n// ERROR\nprint(name)  // Use of unresolved identifier 'name'\n\n// FIX\nlet name = \"Alice\"\nprint(name)\n```\n\n2. **Scope issues**\n```swift\n// ERROR\nif condition {\n    let value = 10\n}\nprint(value)  // value not in scope\n\n// FIX\nlet value: Int\nif condition {\n    value = 10\n} else {\n    value = 0\n}\nprint(value)\n```\n\n3. **Typo in name**\n```swift\nlet userName = \"Alice\"\nprint(username)  // Wrong case\n\n// Fix: Match exact name\nprint(userName)\n```\n\n**Debugging Steps:**\n1. Check spelling and case\n2. Verify scope\n3. Ensure declaration before use\n4. Check import statements"}

# COMPARISON EXAMPLES
{"instruction": "struct vs class swift", "output": "**Struct vs Class in Swift**\n\n| Feature | Struct | Class |\n|---------|--------|-------|\n| Type | Value | Reference |\n| Copying | Copied | Shared |\n| Inheritance | ❌ | ✅ |\n| Deinit | ❌ | ✅ |\n| Mutability | Explicit | Implicit |\n\n**Struct Example:**\n```swift\nstruct Point {\n    var x: Int\n    var y: Int\n}\n\nvar p1 = Point(x: 0, y: 0)\nvar p2 = p1\np2.x = 10\n// p1.x is still 0 (copied)\n```\n\n**Class Example:**\n```swift\nclass Counter {\n    var count = 0\n}\n\nlet c1 = Counter()\nlet c2 = c1\nc2.count = 10\n// c1.count is also 10 (shared)\n```\n\n**When to use:**\n\n**Struct:**\n✓ Data models\n✓ Values without identity\n✓ SwiftUI views\n\n**Class:**\n✓ Shared mutable state\n✓ Inheritance needed\n✓ Identity matters"}

{"instruction": "compare python vs javascript", "output": "**Python vs JavaScript**\n\n**Syntax:**\n```python\n# Python\ndef greet(name):\n    return f\"Hello, {name}\"\n```\n```javascript\n// JavaScript\nfunction greet(name) {\n    return `Hello, ${name}`;\n}\n```\n\n**Key Differences:**\n\n| Feature | Python | JavaScript |\n|---------|--------|------------|\n| Typing | Dynamic | Dynamic |\n| Primary Use | Backend, Data Science | Frontend, Backend |\n| Execution | Interpreted | Interpreted/JIT |\n| Async | async/await | Promises, async/await |\n| Packages | pip | npm |\n\n**Python Strengths:**\n✓ Data science and ML\n✓ Clean, readable syntax\n✓ Scientific computing\n✓ Scripting and automation\n\n**JavaScript Strengths:**\n✓ Web development\n✓ Runs in browser\n✓ Full-stack possible\n✓ Large ecosystem\n\n**Choose based on:**\n• Python for data/ML/scripting\n• JavaScript for web development"}

# BEST PRACTICES
{"instruction": "swift error handling best practices", "output": "**Swift Error Handling Best Practices**\n\n**1. Define Custom Errors**\n```swift\nenum NetworkError: Error {\n    case invalidURL\n    case noConnection\n    case timeout\n    case serverError(Int)\n}\n```\n\n**2. Use throws for propagation**\n```swift\nfunc fetchData() throws -> Data {\n    guard let url = URL(string: urlString) else {\n        throw NetworkError.invalidURL\n    }\n    // ... fetch logic\n}\n```\n\n**3. Handle with do-catch**\n```swift\ndo {\n    let data = try fetchData()\n    process(data)\n} catch NetworkError.invalidURL {\n    print(\"Invalid URL\")\n} catch NetworkError.noConnection {\n    print(\"No internet\")\n} catch {\n    print(\"Unknown error: \\(error)\")\n}\n```\n\n**4. Use Result for async**\n```swift\nfunc fetchData(completion: @escaping (Result<Data, Error>) -> Void) {\n    // ... async work\n    completion(.success(data))\n    // or\n    completion(.failure(error))\n}\n```\n\n**Best Practices:**\n✓ Meaningful error types\n✓ Descriptive messages\n✓ Handle all cases\n✓ Don't swallow errors"}

